      "text": " ... (starts with \"In a typical ...\")</think>In a typical benchmark where a model server reports high tokens per second (TPS) on repeated prompts but much lower speed on unique prompts, the discrepancy usually stems from the difference between **fresh-response throughput** (the rate at which the model generates new tokens for a prompt it has never seen before) and **cached or warmed-throughput** (the rate at which the model reuses tokens from a previously generated sequence that shares a prefix with the current prompt). The high TPS on repeated prompts often reflects the model\u2019s ability to continue generating tokens from a long context without re-computing the entire sequence, whereas the low speed on unique prompts may be caused by the model having to re-enter the full context or by the system\u2019s caching layer serving a partial response that is then extended, which can introduce latency and reduce effective throughput.\n\nPlausible causes include:\n\n- **Prefix reuse**: If the model is prompted with a long system message or few-shot example that is reused across multiple requests, the model can generate tokens by continuing the sequence rather than re-computing the entire prompt. This leads to high TPS on repeated prompts because the model is effectively \u201cwarm\u201d on that prefix.\n- **Caching of intermediate states**: Some serving infrastructures cache the output of a model for a given prompt or a similar prompt. When a unique prompt arrives, the server might first serve a cached response (which is fast) and then extend it, but the extension step can be slower because it must recompute the model\u2019s output for the new tokens.\n- **Context length limits**: If the model\u2019s context window is large, repeated prompts may stay within the limit, allowing the model to generate tokens continuously. Unique prompts that push the context beyond the limit may trigger truncation or re-computation, reducing speed.\n- **System-level optimizations**: Some servers use speculative decoding or other techniques that are more effective on repeated prompts because the model can predict the next tokens more accurately when the prefix is known. On unique prompts, these optimizations may not apply, leading to lower TPS.\n- **Batching and scheduling**: If the server batches requests, the throughput reported may be an average over a batch that includes both repeated and unique prompts. The high TPS on repeated prompts might be masked by the lower speed of unique prompts in the overall average.\n\nTo separate valid fresh-response throughput from warmed or cached throughput, you should:\n\n1. **Instrument the",
